{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"link sink link sink","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md","fileName":"Link Sink.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Link Sink","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[PageRank and Link Analysis]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["PageRank and Link Analysis","Dangling Node","PageRank Teleportation","algorithms.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"link sink link sink","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md","fileName":"Link Sink.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Link Sink","heading":"Link Sink","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A link sink is a group of web-graph nodes with incoming links from outside but no outgoing path back to the rest of the graph. Once a path enters the group, its links keep movement trapped within the group.\n\nIn PageRank, a link sink can absorb rank even when no individual [[Dangling Node]] exists. [[PageRank Teleportation]] gives the random surfer a probability of jumping anywhere, preventing a closed group from draining importance from every other page.","tags":["baby"],"links":["PageRank and Link Analysis","Dangling Node","PageRank Teleportation","algorithms.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"link sink link sink","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Link Sink.md","fileName":"Link Sink.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Link Sink","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":